WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.350
Film Courage: Advice for people at a large
audition, how are you conducting yourself?

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:09.560
How are you staying in your own space when
you can see all the other actors that “Ooohh,

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:11.410
they’re going to get the job over me.”

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:14.910
And the old tapes are going in the head.

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:18.660
How do you stay in your own zone, how do you
focus, how do you conduct yourself?

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:27.730
Robin Riker, Actor: For one thing I never
try to start any conversation with any other

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:28.730
actor.

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:31.630
There was an actress that I don’t see around
so much but I used to see anymore but I used

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:32.630
to see her a lot.

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:36.760
And she worked a lot and I worked a lot and
she knows I do.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.500
And when we see each other at an audition,
it could be me, it could be her.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:43.940
It could be one of the other 10 people if
you’re talking about the big one and she

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:49.630
always starts talking to me and I mean she’s
not a bad person but we’re not friends by

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:50.750
any stretch of the imagination.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:55.080
And I’m not really interested in her life
and I’m sure she’s not interested in mine.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:01:03.200
But she “chats me up” and it’s a way
of kind of distracting me from what it is

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.460
that I need to focus on.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:10.579
So there’s a process and actors should respect
the process.

00:01:10.579 --> 00:01:13.409
You don’t go in chatting away.

00:01:13.409 --> 00:01:16.749
You don’t talk to other actors in full voice.

00:01:16.749 --> 00:01:21.490
If you have someone that you know that you’ve
seen again you give them a hug or whatever

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:25.240
and then you give them their space.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.249
And you do not make phone calls.

00:01:27.249 --> 00:01:32.749
You do not make a lot of noise in the room.

00:01:32.749 --> 00:01:39.481
I always try to find a place off (walking
the hallway or whatever).

00:01:39.481 --> 00:01:45.189
I like to stay on my feet rather than sit
for the most part.

00:01:45.189 --> 00:01:48.899
Sometimes the area where you’re waiting
is kind of restrictive but I always try to

00:01:48.899 --> 00:01:54.729
find a space of my own.

00:01:54.729 --> 00:01:59.229
One thing that is hard to get rid of are the
tapes that run in your head (that you referred

00:01:59.229 --> 00:02:00.899
to) that “Oh they’re going to get it.

00:02:00.899 --> 00:02:02.579
Oh this or that.”

00:02:02.579 --> 00:02:05.399
You do not know.

00:02:05.399 --> 00:02:10.530
None of us know who is going to get it or
why they got it.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:17.150
So many times you get the job because you
look the part.

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:23.010
And sometimes it’s decided before you even
open your mouth in the audition and three

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:25.099
people in the room said “Well that…no.

00:02:25.099 --> 00:02:27.060
That’s not who I see.”

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.540
You could be could be brilliant.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:34.250
You could be Meryl Streep with your talent
level and you just don’t look like what

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:36.239
someone has their mind set on.

00:02:36.239 --> 00:02:38.280
You’re not in the family of looks.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:39.920
But you didn’t get it.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:45.549
But you didn’t get it before you spoke so
it wasn’t your work, it wasn’t your audition.

00:02:45.549 --> 00:02:48.280
It was something else.

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:53.400
It was very discouraging though on those days
you walk in and everybody is dressed exactly

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:58.930
like you are (everybody in the room) and you
see people who have done this before, people

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:02.030
who are more experience than you or less.

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:11.110
But the audition is a real mind scrambler
for many, many actors even very, very talented

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:12.110
actors.

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:19.909
Dustin Hoffman, there’s a famous story for
him auditioning for THE GRADUATE.

00:03:19.909 --> 00:03:27.060
And when he does the seduction scene with
Mrs. Robinson, that little [imitates whimpering]

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:34.069
sound that he made, was the sound he made
in his audition because he was so nervous.

00:03:34.069 --> 00:03:41.879
But they liked what he did and they used it.

00:03:41.879 --> 00:03:48.770
Auditioning is a very hard thing because we
put everything on it, we put all our eggs

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:51.180
in that basket.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:54.640
So it’s a difficult situation.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.579
But in order to make it you just respect the
process.

00:03:58.579 --> 00:04:01.319
You let the other actors do their thing.

00:04:01.319 --> 00:04:07.739
If somebody it talking and you feel like it,
ask them to quiet down.

00:04:07.739 --> 00:04:13.819
Or if you leave the room and you can, that’s
fine.

00:04:13.819 --> 00:04:18.799
But I often see a couple of actors who haven’t
seen each other for awhile and they are just

00:04:18.799 --> 00:04:22.990
catching up and chatting like they’re having
coffee and it messes with everybody in the

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:29.990
room because we’re focusing on what it is
that we want to do and we want to make sure

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:32.180
that we get that done.

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:37.250
So again, it’s just common courtesy.

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:41.740
You used a phrase that I wished I used and
I think I actually used it in the book “the

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:46.920
acceptable level of discourtesy” that is
so prevalent in this town.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:53.210
And so do unto others as they would have them
do unto you.

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:54.240
Be quiet.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:56.919
Let them have their space.

00:04:56.919 --> 00:05:00.210
Don’t muck about with it.

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:02.050
Just do your job.

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:06.780
Film Courage: And then with the casting director,
how are you conducting yourself?

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:10.259
Suppose they are like “Okay, Robin next…”

00:05:10.259 --> 00:05:12.240
And you come in, slate your name.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:17.259
Robin: Well I always like to try and ask a
question.

00:05:17.259 --> 00:05:23.800
Even if you don’t even have a question,
it allows you to be yourself and say “What

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:24.800
about this moment?”

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:27.090
And they get to see you.

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:28.860
Now here are you going for?

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:36.030
Is she really…does she mean don’t come
back again or not?”

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:40.930
Ask them something so they see a little bit
of who you are naturally.

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:44.139
And it breaks the ice a little bit in the
room and you’re not just the dancing monkey

00:05:44.139 --> 00:05:49.050
that comes in to do the, to behave for them,
to perform for them.

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:53.720
So you take a little of the space for yourself
and do your thing.

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:56.789
Film Courage: And then it’s over.

00:05:56.789 --> 00:06:00.590
Are you thanking them and you’re turning
on your heel and you’re out the door?

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:01.590
Robin: Yes.

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:02.590
Film Courage: Okay.

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:03.590
No chit chat?

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:04.729
Robin: No…no.

00:06:04.729 --> 00:06:07.639
If they start something, I’ll say something.

00:06:07.639 --> 00:06:11.280
Sometimes I say “Do you want to see it any
other way?”

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:17.510
But then who told me, one of my New York agents
told me, "Don’t ask them that.

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:21.990
It implies that maybe you didn’t do it right
the first time.”

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:29.520
So I must admit, I’ve dialed back on the
“Do you want to see it any other way?”

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:31.640
But I still ask the question sometimes.

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:34.410
But it’s generally “Thank you very much.”

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:39.830
But I still do sometimes “Do you want to
see anything else?” because I love to get

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:41.780
direction, I love it.

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:47.789
And listen as an actor if someone says “I’d
love to see it a different way” count your

00:06:47.789 --> 00:06:50.461
lucky stars because that’s a fabulous thing.

00:06:50.461 --> 00:06:55.759
Because if you can show facility for taking
direction, if you can change what you did

00:06:55.759 --> 00:07:06.289
and follow direction, you just risen a particular
peg on their peg pole of what you have to

00:07:06.289 --> 00:07:07.289
offer.

00:07:07.289 --> 00:07:12.000
Film Courage: Right because they realize that
that is going to be you on set.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:16.009
Robin: Right and that you can see things another
way.

00:07:16.009 --> 00:07:23.199
And sometimes they say “Do you have any
questions?”

00:07:23.199 --> 00:07:28.093
And if I’m pretty solid in the selections
that I made I’ll say “No, I just want

00:07:28.093 --> 00:07:32.259
you to know that I’ve made a choice about
how I’m going to do it but if you want to

00:07:32.259 --> 00:07:35.240
see it any other way, I’m happy to do that.”

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:37.860
And then I do my thing.

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:40.220
Sometimes they ask to see it another way and
sometimes they don’t.

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:42.819
And sometimes they get the job and sometimes
I don’t.

